    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1001">

                <pb facs="1245" n="1227" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg1227"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112A E W/3–1246: Airgram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Pawley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1946-03-12"
                            >March 12, 1946</date>.<lb/>[Received March 21—11:47 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–146. Reference circular secret airgram of March
                    4, 1946, 9:25 a.m.<anchor n="45" xml:id="d1001-f1"/> requesting a report on the
                    current status of each of the spearhead firms.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Appproximately one dozen firms on the prevailing List are still operating, but
                    all of them are firms locally recognized as Peruvian or as of friendly nations
                    (such as Mandl<anchor n="46" xml:id="d1001-f2"/>), against whom drastic action
                    never has been taken and quite certainly never will be. Apart from these
                    so-called Peruvian firms or firms of friendly nations, the prestige of the
                    Proclaimed List remains comparatively high, but of course with the tendency to
                    pay less and less attention to it as the war feeling recedes. The Embassy is of
                    the opinion that complete withdrawal of the Proclaimed List on May 8 will
                    definitely be in the best interests of both countries.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pawley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1001-f1" n="45" xml:id="d1001-f1-content">
